## End-to-End Flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Browser as Browser (Client)
    participant Client as AGUIChatClient
    participant Server as AG-UI Endpoint
    participant Agent as Agent + LLM
    participant Store as SessionStore

    Browser->>Client: User message
    Client->>Server: POST /agent {thread_id, messages, tools: [client_tools]}

    Server->>Store: GetOrCreateSessionAsync(thread_id)
    Store-->>Server: session (with prior history)

    Server->>Agent: RunStreamingAsync(messages, session, tools)
    Note over Server,Agent: Client tools registered as<br/>declaration-only placeholders

    Agent-->>Server: Stream: text deltas
    Server-->>Client: SSE: TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT

    Agent-->>Server: Stream: tool_calls [get_weather, get_time]
    Note over Server: FilterServerToolsFromMixedToolInvocationsAsync<br/>(get_weather = client, get_time = server)
    Server-->>Client: SSE: TOOL_CALL (get_weather only)
    Note over Server: Server executes get_time internally

    Client->>Client: Execute get_weather locally
    Client->>Server: POST /agent {thread_id, tool_result: get_weather}

    Server->>Store: SaveSessionAsync(thread_id, session)
    Server-->>Client: SSE: final response + RUN_FINISHED
    Client-->>Browser: Render response
```

**Key invariants:**
- **Session continuity** is guaranteed by `thread_id` → `AgentSessionStore` lookup on every request.
- **Tool isolation** is enforced by name-based sets: the server filters its own tool calls from the SSE stream when client tools are present; the client wraps server tool calls in a distinct content type (`server_function_call` / `ServerFunctionCallContent`) so its function invocation layer ignores them.
- **The LLM sees all tools** — both client and server definitions are sent to the model so it can choose freely. The separation is purely at the transport/execution boundary.

